Yes. I inspected the project without modifying it. The deployment should use:

- Angular build stage
- Express/Node production container
- PostgreSQL container with persistent storage
- Caddy container for HTTPS
- Only ports `80` and `443` exposed publicly

Caddy will automatically request and renew HTTPS certificates when the domain points to the VM and ports `80`/`443` are reachable. [Caddy automatic HTTPS](https://caddyserver.com/docs/automatic-https)

## 1. Create these files manually

Create these files in the project root.

### `Dockerfile.deploy`

```dockerfile
FROM node:22-bookworm-slim AS frontend-build

WORKDIR /build/frontend

COPY frontend/package*.json ./
RUN npm ci

COPY frontend/ ./
RUN npm run build

FROM node:22-bookworm-slim AS app

ENV NODE_ENV=production
ENV PORT=3000

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci --omit=dev

COPY . .

COPY --from=frontend-build /build/frontend/dist ./frontend/dist

USER node

EXPOSE 3000

HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=45s --retries=5 \
  CMD node -e "fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/').then(r => process.exit(r.ok ? 0 : 1)).catch(() => process.exit(1))"

CMD ["node", "bootstrap.js"]
```

Your existing Express code serves the Angular output from:

```text
frontend/dist/frontend/browser
```

The Dockerfile preserves that path.

### `.dockerignore`

```text
.git
.gitignore
.env
.env.*
node_modules
frontend/node_modules
frontend/dist
dist
sessions
.agents
.codex
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*
caddy
README.md
```

Do not exclude any application source directories such as `database`, `routers`, `frontend/src`, or `frontend/public`.

### `docker-compose.production.yml`

```yaml
services:
  postgres:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    restart: unless-stopped

    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: ${DB_NAME:?DB_NAME is required}
      POSTGRES_USER: ${DB_USER:?DB_USER is required}
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:?DB_PASSWORD is required}

    volumes:
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U $${POSTGRES_USER} -d $${POSTGRES_DB}"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 10
      start_period: 30s

  app:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile.deploy

    restart: unless-stopped

    env_file:
      - .env.production

    environment:
      NODE_ENV: production
      PORT: 3000
      DB_HOST: postgres
      DB_PORT: 5432

    expose:
      - "3000"

    depends_on:
      postgres:
        condition: service_healthy

  caddy:
    image: caddy:2-alpine
    restart: unless-stopped

    ports:
      - "80:80"
      - "443:443"

    volumes:
      - ./caddy:/etc/caddy:ro
      - caddy_data:/data
      - caddy_config:/config

    depends_on:
      app:
        condition: service_healthy

volumes:
  postgres_data:
  caddy_data:
  caddy_config:
```

The PostgreSQL healthcheck prevents the application from starting before PostgreSQL is ready. This is the recommended Compose pattern for database dependencies. [Docker Compose startup ordering](https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/startup-order/)

### `caddy/Caddyfile`

Create a `caddy` directory, then create this file inside it:

```text
geoguessing.me {
    encode gzip zstd

    reverse_proxy app:3000
}
```

Do not use `localhost:3000` in the Caddyfile. Inside Docker, `localhost` means the Caddy container itself. `app:3000` refers to the Express container.

The Caddy data and configuration volumes are important because Caddy stores certificates and TLS state in `/data`. [Caddy Docker image documentation](https://hub.docker.com/_/caddy)

### `.env.production`

Create this file manually on the VM. Do not commit it or upload your existing local `.env`.

```dotenv
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production

DB_NAME=geoguesser
DB_USER=geoguesser_app
DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_PASSWORD
DB_HOST=postgres
DB_PORT=5432

MAPILLARY_ACCESS_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_MAPILLARY_TOKEN

GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
GEMINI_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_GEMINI_KEY

JWT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_SECRET

FRONTEND_URL=https://geoguessing.me

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL=https://geoguessing.me/auth/google/callback

STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=REPLACE_WITH_STRIPE_PRICE_ID
```

Generate safe random values on the VM with:

```bash
openssl rand -hex 32
openssl rand -hex 64
```

Use the generated values for `DB_PASSWORD` and `JWT_SECRET`.

## 2. Configure DNS

Your existing DNS should have:

```text
geoguessing.me  A  99.79.152.126
```

Before starting Caddy, verify it:

```bash
dig +short geoguessing.me
```

It should return:

```text
99.79.152.126
```

If there is an incorrect `AAAA` record, remove it or update it. An incorrect IPv6 record can cause certificate issuance to fail.

The Caddyfile intentionally does not include `www.geoguessing.me`. Add a separate `www` DNS record and Caddy hostname later if you want that alias.

## 3. Configure Google OAuth

In Google Cloud Console, add:

```text
Authorized JavaScript origin:
https://geoguessing.me
```

```text
Authorized redirect URI:
https://geoguessing.me/auth/google/callback
```

The callback URL must exactly match `GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL`.

## 4. Configure Stripe

Create or update the Stripe webhook endpoint:

```text
https://geoguessing.me/api/webhook
```

Enable the events your application handles, especially:

```text
checkout.session.completed
customer.subscription.deleted
```

Copy the resulting webhook signing secret into:

```dotenv
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...
```

Also make sure your Stripe price ID is correct.

Before deployment, rotate any credential-like Stripe key currently present in `README.md` or elsewhere in the repository. Do not upload your local `.env`.

## 5. Prepare the VM

The commands below assume Ubuntu 22.04 or Ubuntu 24.04.

SSH into the VM:

```bash
ssh YOUR_VM_USERNAME@99.79.152.126
```

Update the VM:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

Install Docker from Docker’s official repository:

```bash
sudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl

sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings

sudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg \
  -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc

sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc

echo \
  "Types: deb
URIs: https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu
Suites: $(. /etc/os-release && echo "${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-$VERSION_CODENAME}")
Components: stable
Architectures: $(dpkg --print-architecture)
Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc" \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources > /dev/null

sudo apt update

sudo apt install -y \
  docker-ce \
  docker-ce-cli \
  containerd.io \
  docker-buildx-plugin \
  docker-compose-plugin
```

Docker officially recommends the Compose plugin, which uses the command `docker compose`, not the older `docker-compose` command. [Docker Ubuntu installation](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/)

Enable Docker:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo docker run hello-world
```

Optionally allow your normal user to run Docker without `sudo`:

```bash
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
```

Log out and reconnect:

```bash
exit
ssh YOUR_VM_USERNAME@99.79.152.126
```

Verify:

```bash
docker --version
docker compose version
```

## 6. Configure the VM firewall

Allow SSH, HTTP, and HTTPS:

```bash
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw --force enable
sudo ufw status
```

Also configure the VM provider’s network firewall/security group:

```text
TCP 22   only from your own IP if possible
TCP 80   from anywhere
TCP 443  from anywhere
```

Do not open these publicly:

```text
TCP 3000
TCP 5432
```

The Compose file does not publish either of those ports.

## 7. Copy the project to the VM

Create a deployment directory:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /opt/geoguesser
sudo chown -R "$USER":"$USER" /opt/geoguesser
```

From your local machine, copy the project while excluding local dependencies, build output, and secrets:

```bash
rsync -az \
  --exclude '.git' \
  --exclude '.env' \
  --exclude '.env.*' \
  --exclude 'node_modules' \
  --exclude 'frontend/node_modules' \
  --exclude 'frontend/dist' \
  ./ \
  YOUR_VM_USERNAME@99.79.152.126:/opt/geoguesser/
```

Then return to the VM:

```bash
cd /opt/geoguesser
```

Create the Caddy directory if necessary:

```bash
mkdir -p caddy
```

Create `.env.production`:

```bash
nano .env.production
```

Paste the production environment variables from above, then protect the file:

```bash
chmod 600 .env.production
```

## 8. Validate the configuration

Validate the Compose file:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  config -q
```

Validate Caddy:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  run --rm --no-deps caddy \
  validate \
  --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile \
  --adapter caddyfile
```

Build the application image:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  build --pull app
```

## 9. Start the deployment

Start all services:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  up -d
```

Check the containers:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  ps
```

You should see:

```text
postgres   healthy
app        healthy
caddy      running
```

Review logs:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  logs --tail=100 postgres app caddy
```

The application’s existing startup code runs:

```text
sequelize.authenticate()
sequelize.sync({ alter: true })
```

Therefore, a new PostgreSQL volume should be initialized automatically by the application. Do not use `docker compose down -v` unless you intentionally want to delete the database volume.

## 10. Test HTTPS

From the VM:

```bash
curl -I http://geoguessing.me
```

The HTTP request should redirect to HTTPS.

Then:

```bash
curl -I https://geoguessing.me
```

The HTTPS request should return a successful response.

Test the unauthenticated API:

```bash
curl -i https://geoguessing.me/api/me
```

A `401 Unauthorized` response is expected because there is no login cookie.

Then open:

```text
https://geoguessing.me
```

Test:

1. The Angular page loads.
2. Google login redirects correctly.
3. The callback returns to `https://geoguessing.me`.
4. The login cookie is created.
5. Subscription checkout redirects correctly.
6. Stripe webhook delivery succeeds.
7. A subscribed user can access the game.
8. An unauthenticated user cannot access protected routes.

## 11. Updating the deployment later

After copying updated source files:

```bash
cd /opt/geoguesser

docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  build app

docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  up -d app
```

If the Caddyfile changes:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  exec -w /etc/caddy caddy \
  caddy reload \
  --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile \
  --adapter caddyfile
```

If the deployment needs to be stopped temporarily:

```bash
docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  down
```

This stops the containers but preserves PostgreSQL data because the named volume remains.

## 12. Back up PostgreSQL

Before significant changes, create a backup:

```bash
cd /opt/geoguesser

set -a
. ./.env.production
set +a

mkdir -p backups

docker compose \
  --env-file .env.production \
  -f docker-compose.production.yml \
  exec -T postgres \
  pg_dump -U "$DB_USER" -d "$DB_NAME" \
  | gzip > "backups/geoguesser-$(date +%Y-%m-%d-%H%M%S).sql.gz"
```

One final application check: production frontend requests should use relative URLs such as `/api/me`, not `http://localhost:3000/api/me`. The Angular proxy is for local development only; in production, Caddy and Express serve everything under the same HTTPS domain.

