Yes. Your code already contains the Google OAuth implementation, so you only need to configure:

1. A Google Cloud OAuth client.
2. The correct environment variables.
3. Your production HTTPS callback URL.

The relevant code reads `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL`, then registers these routes:

```text
/auth/google
/auth/google/callback
```

Your code requests only Google `profile` and `email` information.

## 1. Get access to the Google Cloud project

You have two options:

- Use your group member’s existing Google Cloud project. They must grant your Google account access to that project.
- Create your own Google Cloud project and create a new OAuth client there.

For a group project, using the existing project is usually easiest. Ask the project owner to add your Google account through:

```text
Google Cloud Console
→ IAM & Admin
→ IAM
→ Grant access
```

You need permission to configure Google Auth Platform and create OAuth clients.

## 2. Open Google Auth Platform

Go to:

[Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/)

Then:

```text
Select the correct project
→ Google Auth Platform
→ Overview
→ Get started
```

Google’s newer interface calls this “Google Auth Platform.” Older projects may show equivalent options under:

```text
APIs & Services
→ OAuth consent screen
APIs & Services
→ Credentials
```

## 3. Configure Branding

Open:

```text
Google Auth Platform
→ Branding
```

Enter values similar to these:

```text
App name:
GeoGuesser

User support email:
your email address

Developer contact email:
your email address
```

Under authorized domains, add:

```text
geoguessing.me
```

For the homepage, use:

```text
https://geoguessing.me
```

If you have privacy-policy and terms-of-service pages, add them as well.

Google requires production apps to use verified domains and may require homepage/privacy-policy information for verification. [Google OAuth branding requirements](https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/15549049)

If Google asks you to verify ownership of `geoguessing.me`, use Google Search Console and add the requested DNS TXT record to your domain.

## 4. Configure the audience

Open:

```text
Google Auth Platform
→ Audience
```

Choose:

```text
User type:
External
```

Use `External` if people outside your school or organization need to log in.

For initial testing, leave the app in:

```text
Publishing status:
Testing
```

Then add your Google account and your group members under:

```text
Test users
```

Every person who tests the application should be added there.

Google currently limits testing-mode apps to up to 100 test users. [Google app audience documentation](https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/15549945)

## 5. Configure the requested data access

Open:

```text
Google Auth Platform
→ Data access
```

Add only the basic sign-in scopes:

```text
Email
Profile
```

Do not add Google Drive, Gmail, Calendar, Sheets, or other scopes. Your current code does not request them.

If Google displays the underlying scope names, they may appear as:

```text
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/userinfo.email
https://www.googleapis.com/auth/userinfo.profile
```

If Google automatically includes `openid` as part of its sign-in configuration, that is acceptable. Do not manually add unrelated scopes.

## 6. Create the OAuth client

Open:

```text
Google Auth Platform
→ Clients
→ Create client
```

Choose:

```text
Application type:
Web application
```

Use a name such as:

```text
GeoGuesser Production
```

Under **Authorized JavaScript origins**, add exactly:

```text
https://geoguessing.me
```

Under **Authorized redirect URIs**, add exactly:

```text
https://geoguessing.me/auth/google/callback
```

Do not add a trailing slash.

The callback URL must match the application’s configured URL exactly, including:

- `https` versus `http`
- capitalization
- hostname
- path
- trailing slash

Otherwise Google returns `redirect_uri_mismatch`. [Google OAuth redirect URI requirements](https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/web-server)

Click **Create**.

Copy both values immediately:

```text
Client ID
Client secret
```

Google now only shows the full client secret when it is created. Store it securely. [Google OAuth client documentation](https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/15549257)

## 7. Put the values in your production environment file

On the VM, edit:

```bash
nano /opt/geoguesser/.env.production
```

Set these values:

```dotenv
NODE_ENV=production

FRONTEND_URL=https://geoguessing.me

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=PASTE_YOUR_CLIENT_ID_HERE
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=PASTE_YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET_HERE
GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL=https://geoguessing.me/auth/google/callback
```

Do not put the client secret in JavaScript source code, Git, the README, or the Angular frontend.

